The attached image, Figure 3 from the npj Vaccines (2017) paper, illustrates that Nano-11 induces transient inflammation at the injection site and is taken up by recruited cells. Panel A shows the kinetics of various inflammatory cells, including Mac-2+ macrophages and Ly6C+ monocytes, peaking at the injection site around day 2, while neutrophils show an early, sharp peak and decline. Panel B, using confocal microscopy, provides visual evidence of Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 (red) inside MHCII+, Ly6C+, and Mac-2+ immune cells (green) from 6 hours to day 7 post-injection, confirming cellular uptake. These findings support that Nano-11's adjuvant effect involves local immune cell recruitment and phagocytosis, which resolves over time.